ies that support the commander’s intent. Effectively communicate during planning, preparation, and execution. Publicly praise good performance and privately reprimand substandard performance. Operations: Assess the operational environment, leveraging knowledge and experience to understand how it will affect accomplishment of the unit’s mission. Employ the principles of Mission Command to support the commander’s intent. Advise the platoon leader and commander on prudent and acceptable levels of risk during training and operations. Program Management: Conduct continuous performance assessments to ensure the platoon meets the unit mission requirements. Increase maintenance program efficiency by understanding and being actively involved in platoon level maintenance. Ensure the platoon’s individual and collective training records are accurately recorded in the proper system of record ATIS. Professional Reading: AR 623-3 (Evaluation and Reporting System) AR 600-100 (Army Profession and Leadership Policy) STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks, Warrior Leader Skills Level 2, 3, and 4) AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy) TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1, Skill Level 2 ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession) FM 7-0 (Training) ADP 7-0 (Training) ADP 3-19 (Fires) FM 3-0 (Operations) AR 350-1 (Army Training and Leader Development) Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Senior Leaders Course (SLC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS competencies and is vital for preparing yourself and improving your combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: Completing an associate degree or at least two years of college credit is an excellent way to separate yourself from your peers. For assistance with your educational goals, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) MG Aubrey “Red” Newman Award Larry L. Strickland Educational Leadership Award (SELA) Edmond L. Gruber Award NCO of the Month (Battalion) NCO of the Quarter (Brigade) NCO of the Year (Division) Sergeant Audie Murphy Club (SAMC) Sergeant Morales Club Mentorship & Career Guidance: Establish relationships based on mutual trust and respect. This allows leaders to establish a mentorship role to advise and guide both personal and professional growth. Use ADP 6-22 to refine the mentorship process. To provide strategic career guidance to all subordinate enlisted Soldiers within your formation, reference the information in this pamphlet at each appropriate level. Advise section leadership on talent management, ensuring the right leaders are placed in the right positions to maximize organizational effectiveness. Your role in professional counseling is critical for shaping the future of the NCO Corps.', 'cb23c96416e217409d883c03e10dddf17d411d60429512c8cd220978a952c509', 8, 11, null, 27, null, null, false, null),
  ('92549e90-9289-5980-93c5-8bdab2546b33', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13R', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The MSG serves as a key enlisted member of staff elements at battalion or higher levels. The MSG’s level of experience makes them an invaluable member of the staff for input on policy development, training development, and enforcing standards and discipline within the organization. The 1SG is the senior enlisted advisor to the commander at the company/troop/battery level. 1SGs are responsible for maintaining and enforcing standards, ensuring training objectives are met, developing the Soldiers in the unit, all administrative functions, and ensuring the health, welfare, and morale of the unit and their Families. The 1SG serves as a coordinator for training and resources, as well as the lead integrator with outside organizations and entities. Core Competencies: Readiness: Assist the commander in prioritizing AR 350-1 training; enforce the unit’s training program. Advise the commander on Uniform Code of Military Justice actions and manage the execution of the administration process. Implement and manage the unit physical readiness program. Leadership: Assess, create, and foster an ethical and positive culture and climate within the organization. Communicate and drive the commander’s vision and intent by serving as the conduit between the commander and Soldiers. Coach and foster servant leadership through effective counseling and development. Training Management: Design, implement, and manage an NCO leader development program and coach junior officers. Provide oversight of the training management process. Monitor and evaluate individual and collective training in accordance with the unit’s approved METL. Communication: Utilize both oral and written products to effectively communicate ideas, viewpoints, and military correspondence. Employ active listening and the components of communication to solve problems and achieve results. Exercise and enforce emotional intelligence (self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, and social skill). Operations: Advise commanders and staff; direct the NCO support channel in all operations. Extend the operational reach of the commander in all aspects, from leadership to planning and operations. Analyze operational gaps in both the organization and the plan; leverage the shortfalls by providing the appropriate resources and guidance to achieve positive results. Program Management: Train and educate subordinates on Army and organizational programs. Execute talent management. Assist and enforce the standards associated with unit maintenance programs. Professional Reading: AR 623-3 (Evaluation and Reporting System) AR 600-100 (Army Profession and Leadership Policy) STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks, Warrior Leader Skills Level 2, 3, and 4) AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy) TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1, Skill Level 2 ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession) FM 7-0 (Training) ADP 7-0 (Training) ADP 3-19 (Fires) FM 3-0 (Operations) AR 350-1 (Army Training and Leader Development) ADP 6-0 (Mission Command: Command And Control Of Army Forces) Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Master Leaders Course (MLC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS competencies are vital for preparing yourself and improving your combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: Completing a bachelor’s degree is an excellent way to separate yourself from your peers. For assistance with your educational goals, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) Larry L Strickland Educational Leadership Award (SELA) Edmund L. Gruber Award Sergeant Audie Murphy Club (SAMC) Sergeant Morales Club Mentorship & Career Guidance: Establish relationships based on mutual trust and respect. This allows leaders to establish a mentorship role to advise and guide both personal and professional growth. Use ADP 6-22 to refine the mentorship process. To provide strategic career guidance to all subordinate enlisted Soldiers within your formation, reference the information in this pamphlet at each appropriate level. Advise section leadership on talent management, ensuring the right leaders are placed in the right positions to maximize organizational effectiveness. Your role in professional counseling is critical for shaping the future of the NCO Corps.', '07a8c68df95bdb0af21561ba3f212329f771f405287693c6165a2d247d6a5b31', 11, 14, null, 28, null, null, false, null),
  ('76e29c8b-b81e-581c-a79e-f6f67ea52a6c', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13R', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The SGM is the key enlisted member of staff elements at battalion and higher levels. The SGM is key in the training and operations planning process, leveraging their experience and vast technical and tactical knowledge to advise the staff and the Operations Officer. The SGM is a subject matter expert in their field, primary advisor on policy development, analytical reviewer of regulatory guidance and often fulfills the duties of the CSM in the incumbent’s absence. The CSM is the senior NCO in the command at battalion and higher levels. The CSM carries out, and enforces, policies and standards on performance, training, appearance and conduct of the organization. The CSM is the principal advisor to the commander, giving advice and making recommendations to the commander and staff on matters pertaining to the organization. The CSM is responsible for enlisted talent management and ensures all aspects of the NCO Common Core Competencies (NCO C3) are present in the operational domain. Core Competencies: Readiness: Determine effectiveness of all policies and practices affecting readiness; manage the process. Manage the personnel and individual training components of the Unit Status Reporting (USR). Manage Human Resources to maintain directed levels of individual readiness. Leadership: Develop agile and adaptive leaders who solve problems using critical and creative thinking to sustain an organizational culture that enables Mission Command. Mentor staff NCOs and officers, continually develop their skills to build a stronger staff and planning cell. Extend influence inside and outside the organization through negotiation and utilizing social intelligence (self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, and social skills). Training Management: Ensure training meets the commander’s intent, mission requirements, and Soldier’s abilities. Foster an organizational learning culture where opportunities for growth and improvement are enduring. Eliminate or minimize training distractions; discipline the training management process. Communication: Utilizes engaging communication techniques and messages to ensure open communication, solicit feedback from the staff, and remains approachable to foster a positive command environment that supports organizational growth through open and candid communication. Engage key stakeholders and foster strong relationships with agencies within the community to build teams and expand the unit’s sphere of influence. Build relationships with peer units and higher headquarters to champion the unit up and out. Operations: Extend the operational reach of the commander in all aspects, from leadership, to planning, and operations. Engage throughout the planning process – Influence planning efforts to reflect reality and true training goals. Ensure leaders are accountable and responsible for the proficiency of their subordinates (Soldiers and Army Civilians) to prepare to conduct its wartime mission. Program Management: Develops, mentors, and manages talent two levels down, based on organizational requirements and assists in subordinates career progression. Shape leaders through talent management efforts that develop NCOs through educational and training opportunities. Enforce effective use of available time to support unit increases in proficiency; actively champion the balance of work/life balance for Soldiers and leaders. Military and Civilian Education: Military Education: Sergeants Major Course (SMC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS specific individual and collective tasks is vital for preparing yourself and improving your unit''s combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: While not a requirement for promotion, possessing a bachelor’s degree and actively working towards a graduate degree is an excellent way to separate yourself from your peers. For assistance with your educational goals, visit your installation''s Education Center. Army National Guard of the United States (ARNGUS) Army National Guard Field Artillery NCOs are essential to the Total Force and must meet the same high standards of proficiency and readiness as their Active Army counterparts. Your career path and training opportunities are designed to parallel the active component, ensuring seamless integration during military operations. Equivalent Standards & Training: ARNGUS NCOs must possess the same qualifications, capabilities, and training as Active Army Field Artillery NCOs. Career progression and duty assignments are structured to mirror those in the active component, ensuring you are prepared for any mission. Primary Mission & Readiness: Your primary peacetime mission is to sustain individual and collective training to maintain a constant state of readiness for deployment and combat operations. Focus on perfecting your combat skills and developing your subordinates into a lethal and effective Field Artillery unit. Professional Development: The ARNGUS Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development System (NCOPDS) fulfils all professional development and functional area requirements. While your unit assignments may be influenced by geography, your professional growth path aligns with the standards of the entire Field Artillery community. For specific career progression details, reference the associated MOS chapter for your active duty counterparts. Dual Role as a Citizen-Soldier: In addition to your federal mission, you serve your state and community. You may be activated by your state’s governor to provide support during natural or man made disasters or to assist in Homeland Security missions.', 'a5bfca8483d937f2cdf931492b27a6841b6959eb8c2c7d7c1d46fe57231d1a63', 14, 17, null, 29, null, null, false, null),
  ('e0dd4259-378e-537a-9d0c-554ab1f01530', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13Z', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'Private to Specialist: Disciplined Field Artillery Soldiers embody the Army Values and the Warrior Ethos. They are constantly training to perfect the technical and tactical skills required to deliver timely and accurate fires. Their commitment to the mission—never quitting, accepting defeat, or leaving a fallen comrade is proven through synchronized teamwork and clear communication across the fires enterprise. They stand ready to deploy and support the maneuver force with overwhelming firepower, ensuring maneuver commanders can close with and destroy the enemy. Core Competencies: Demonstrate a high degree of proficiency in warrior tasks and battle drills. Strive for technical and tactical proficiency. Mastering leadership starts with internalizing Be, Know, Do: embodying the character of a professional, mastering the necessary intellectual skills, and executing the actions required for mission success. BE: Attributes of Character and Presence "Be" represents the internal qualities that define a leader’s identity and how they are perceived by others. • Character: This is the moral and ethical core of a leader. It involves living the Army Values (Loyalty, Duty, Respect, Selfless Service, Honor, Integrity, Personal Courage) and the Warrior Ethos. A leader of character does what is right even when no one is watching, maintaining empathy and humility to support their team''s morale. • Presence: This is the outward appearance and professional image a leader projects. It Includes military bearing, physical fitness, confidence, and resilience. A strong presence ensures a leader is visible, approachable, and sets a positive example that inspires trust in subordinates. KNOW: The Attribute of Intellect "Know" refers to the mental and social capabilities a leader uses to make sound decisions. • Intellect: This encompasses a leader''s brainpower and knowledge base. It involves mental agility—the ability to adapt to changing situations—and sound judgment to solve problems effectively. It also includes interpersonal tact, which allows leaders to build consensus and recognize individual strengths within their team. DO: Competencies of Leads, Develops, and Achieves "Do" describes the specific actions and skills leaders must demonstrate to fulfill their responsibilities. • Leads: This competency involves providing purpose, direction, and motivation. Leaders influence others by communicating effectively, building trust, and leading by example to achieve the mission. • Develops: A leader focuses on improving the organization and its people. This is achieved by creating a positive environment, coaching, mentoring, and ensuring that subordinates are prepared for future roles. • Achieves: This is the ultimate goal of leadership: getting results. Leaders achieve by integrating all other attributes and competencies to execute tasks, meet the commander''s intent, and provide constructive feedback to enhance team performance. Professional Reading: STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1 TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Military Education is crucial for service members as they provide specialized training that enhances MOS competency and builds overall combat effectiveness. By building Soldier proficiencies and fostering tactical knowledge, these educational experiences ensure units are better prepared and more capable. Civilian Education: Planning your college program early is encouraged. To explore your educational opportunities, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) Soldier of the Month (Battalion) Soldier of the Quarter (Brigade) Soldier of the Year (Division) Best Section Best Redleg Competition (Branch)', 'ab84d168844beb5c1e874ffcaa735a3761ad3bad994d016ecadcc6262b0f0682', 1, 3, null, 30, null, null, false, null),
  ('1bfed7ff-5975-52f6-878f-29325179bd28', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13Z', null, 'development', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The CPL was established in 1775 with the birth of the Army and the NCO Corps. Along with the rank of sergeant, the corporal is the only rank that has never disappeared from the NCO Corps. CPLs are the base of the NCO ranks, serving as leaders of the smallest Army units, principally, team leaders. Like sergeants, corporals are responsible for the individual training, personal appearance, and cleanliness of their Soldiers. As the command sergeant major is known as the epitome of success in the NCO Corps, thethe corporal is the beginning of the NCO Corps. As the NCO Corps is known as the backbone of the Army, the corporal and the sergeant are the backbone of the NCO Corps. The Sergeant exercises direct leadership and supervision over assigned teams, functioning as the first line of the NCO Support Channel. As a first line supervisor, SGTs have the most direct impact on Soldiers. SGTs live and work with Soldiers every day and are responsible for their health, welfare, and safety. The counseling, training, and care SGTs provide will determine the success of the unit’s mission and prevent issues from occurring during home station operations. These leaders ensure their Soldiers meet all standards. They teach them to maintain and account for their team members as well as unit equipment. Core Competencies: Readiness: Know your Soldiers and how their individual readiness affects unit readiness. Focus on commander’s key training objectives and own the individual training of your Soldiers that supports readiness. Conduct performance assessments and open and honest counseling of subordinates. Leadership: Demonstrate the courage and moral strength to do the right thing consistent with the Army Values and the Army Ethics. Develop Soldier knowledge, skills, and behaviors. Create and foster an ethical and positive culture within the unit. Training Management: Understand the commander’s intent, mission requirements, and know Soldiers’ abilities to achieve desired training outcomes. Lead effective, challenging, and realistic training. Follow Troop Leading Procedures and the 8-step training model to support the training management process. Professional Reading: AR 623-3 Evaluation and Reporting System AR 600-100 Army Profession and Leadership Policy ADP 6-22 Army Leadership and the Profession STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks, Warrior Leader Skills Level 2, 3, and 4) AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy) TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1, Skill Level 2 Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Basic Leader Course (BLC) is your first step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS competency is vital for preparing yourself and improving combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: Planning your college program early is encouraged. To explore your educational opportunities, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) MG Aubrey “Red” Newman Award NCO of the Month (Battalion) NCO of the Quarter (Brigade) NCO of the Year (Division) Best Section Best Redleg Competition (Branch) Sergeant Audie Murphy Club (SAMC) Sergeant Morales Club Mentorship & Career Guidance: Establish relationships based on mutual trust and respect. This allows leaders to establish a mentorship role to advise and guide both personal and professional growth. Use ADP 6-22 to refine the mentorship process. To provide strategic career guidance to all subordinate enlisted Soldiers within your formation, reference the information in this pamphlet at each appropriate level. Advise section leadership on talent management, ensuring the right leaders are placed in the right positions to maximize organizational effectiveness. Your role in professional counseling is critical for shaping the future of the NCO Corps.', '71cca11b768536e274619f102a76bc3b7c161faaef19e6ce34894fc592fdba1a', 3, 5, null, 31, null, null, false, null),
  ('6f3d80fa-3594-5e1a-9899-26a29f0af79c', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13Z', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The SSG is a critical link in the NCO support channel. As a first line supervisor, SSGs live and work with Soldiers every day and are responsible for their health, welfare, and safety. These leaders ensure that their Soldiers meet standards in personal appearance and teach them to maintain and account for their individual and unit equipment and property. The SSG enforces standards, develops, and trains Soldiers in MOS competencies to accomplish the mission. The SSGs secondary role is to support the chain of command through the NCO Support Channel. Core Competencies: Readiness: Understand, train, and assess individual, team, and squad tasks that fall within unit METL. Develop squad training plans that support the unit METL and are nested with platoon and company training plans. Understand and communicate the importance of readiness, the systems that track it, and the impacts it has on the mission. Leadership: Actions and conduct consistently reflect the Army Values and Army Ethics; remain a role model for Soldiers. Create and foster an ethical and positive culture within the unit. Know your Soldiers at a personal level to promote effective personal and professional counseling. Training Management: Lead tough, realistic, and challenging training. Ensure training meets the commander’s intent, mission requirements, and enhances Soldier’s abilities. Implement and enforce policies and standards on training and emphasize the 8-step training model. Communications: Communicate commander’s intent and operational objectives ensuring a shared understanding by subordinates. Employ active listening to gain a thorough understanding of commander’s intent, unit’s mission, and challenges presented by the operational environment. Effectively using written communication when writing narratives, awards, evaluations, and electronic communications. Operations: Understand the commander’s intent and employ principles of mission command to achieve it in the operational environment. Engaged throughout the planning process, assist in and be a part of the planning, preparation, execution, and assessment of training. Be a trainer to increase proficiency of their subordinates (Soldiers and Army Civilians) to prepare to conduct a unit’s wartime mission. Professional Reading: AR 623-3 (Evaluation and Reporting System) AR 600-100 (Army Profession and Leadership Policy) STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks, Warrior Leader Skills Level 2, 3, and 4) AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy) TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1, Skill Level 2 ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession) FM 7-0 (Training) ADP 7-0 (Training) ADP 3-19 (Fires) Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Advance Leaders Course (ALC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS competencies is vital for preparing yourself and improving your combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: Pursuing an associate degree can set you apart from your peers. To explore this and other educational opportunities, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) MG Aubrey “Red” Newman Award Larry L. Strickland Educational Leadership Award (SELA) Edmond L. Gruber Award Best Section Best Redleg Competition (Branch) NCO of the Month (Battalion) NCO of the Quarter (Brigade) NCO of the Year (Division) Sergeant Audie Murphy Club (SAMC) Sergeant Morales Club Mentorship & Career Guidance: Establish relationships based on mutual trust and respect. This allows leaders to establish a mentorship role to advise and guide both personal and professional growth. Use ADP 6-22 to refine the mentorship process. To provide strategic career guidance to all subordinate enlisted Soldiers within your formation, reference the information in this pamphlet at each appropriate level. Advise section leadership on talent management, ensuring the right leaders are placed in the right positions to maximize organizational effectiveness. Your role in professional counseling is critical for shaping the future of the NCO Corps.', 'b5629471453a2da4d0fbfc97ac2f633238feb60225a05278712cc1a55e077028', 5, 8, null, 32, null, null, false, null),
  ('eeae7698-35fc-536e-a4f9-99ed3509a59a', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The SFC serves at all echelons. The Platoon Sergeant is the senior NCO at the platoon level and primary advisor to the Platoon Leader. They are responsible for the continued professional development and mentoring of the Platoon Leader. The Platoon Sergeant is primarily responsible for the health, welfare, training, and development of the Soldiers in their charge. They are the first level of NCO that has input into the unit''s Mission Essential Task List and train collective tasks at the platoon level. The SFC uses their extensive experience and knowledge base to provide input to their superiors for purposes of planning or executing operations. They may also be called upon to train Soldiers on both collective and individual tasks. Core Competencies: Readiness: Proactively address Soldier issues that will affect unit readiness. Prioritize readiness across the formation by forecasting future shortfalls and gaps created by equipment and personnel changes. Understand and teach the impact of Soldier’s individual readiness on the unit. Leadership: Utilize Mission Command to execute the commander’s and platoon leader’s intent through disciplined initiative empowering subordinate leaders. Coach and counsel the NCOs in the platoon, as well as advising and mentoring the platoon leader. Create and foster an ethical and positive culture within the unit. Training Management: Ensure training is properly planned, resourced, rehearsed, and executed. Set and enforce standards during all training events. Ensure Soldiers know and understand commander’s intent, mission requirements, and the reason for the training event(s). Communications: Provide direction, guidance, and clear priorities that support the commander’s intent. Effectively communicate during planning, preparation, and execution. Publicly praise good performance and privately reprimand substandard performance. Operations: Assess the operational environment, leveraging knowledge and experience to understand how it will affect accomplishment of the unit’s mission. Employ the principles of Mission Command to support the commander’s intent. Advise the platoon leader and commander on prudent and acceptable levels of risk during training and operations. Program Management: Conduct continuous performance assessments to ensure the platoon meets the unit mission requirements. Increase maintenance program efficiency by understanding and being actively involved in platoon level maintenance. Ensure the platoon’s individual and collective training records are accurately recorded in the proper system of record ATIS. Professional Reading: AR 623-3 (Evaluation and Reporting System) AR 600-100 (Army Profession and Leadership Policy) STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks, Warrior Leader Skills Level 2, 3, and 4) AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy) TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1, Skill Level 2 ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession) FM 7-0 (Training) ADP 7-0 (Training) ADP 3-19 (Fires) FM 3-0 (Operations) AR 350-1 (Army Training and Leader Development) Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Senior Leaders Course (SLC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS competencies and is vital for preparing yourself and improving your combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: Completing an associate degree or at least two years of college credit is an excellent way to separate yourself from your peers. For assistance with your educational goals, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) MG Aubrey “Red” Newman Award Larry L. Strickland Educational Leadership Award (SELA) Edmond L. Gruber Award NCO of the Month (Battalion) NCO of the Quarter (Brigade) NCO of the Year (Division) Sergeant Audie Murphy Club (SAMC) Sergeant Morales Club Mentorship & Career Guidance: Establish relationships based on mutual trust and respect. This allows leaders to establish a mentorship role to advise and guide both personal and professional growth. Use ADP 6-22 to refine the mentorship process. To provide strategic career guidance to all subordinate enlisted Soldiers within your formation, reference the information in this pamphlet at each appropriate level. Advise section leadership on talent management, ensuring the right leaders are placed in the right positions to maximize organizational effectiveness. Your role in professional counseling is critical for shaping the future of the NCO Corps.', 'cb23c96416e217409d883c03e10dddf17d411d60429512c8cd220978a952c509', 8, 11, null, 33, null, null, false, null),
  ('d16042be-fffa-52db-9a59-6761786a6bb9', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13Z', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The MSG serves as a key enlisted member of staff elements at battalion or higher levels. The MSG’s level of experience makes them an invaluable member of the staff for input on policy development, training development, and enforcing standards and discipline within the organization. The 1SG is the senior enlisted advisor to the commander at the company/troop/battery level. 1SGs are responsible for maintaining and enforcing standards, ensuring training objectives are met, developing the Soldiers in the unit, all administrative functions, and ensuring the health, welfare, and morale of the unit and their Families. The 1SG serves as a coordinator for training and resources, as well as the lead integrator with outside organizations and entities. Core Competencies: Readiness: Assist the commander in prioritizing AR 350-1 training; enforce the unit’s training program. Advise the commander on Uniform Code of Military Justice actions and manage the execution of the administration process. Implement and manage the unit physical readiness program. Leadership: Assess, create, and foster an ethical and positive culture and climate within the organization. Communicate and drive the commander’s vision and intent by serving as the conduit between the commander and Soldiers. Coach and foster servant leadership through effective counseling and development. Training Management: Design, implement, and manage an NCO leader development program and coach junior officers. Provide oversight of the training management process. Monitor and evaluate individual and collective training in accordance with the unit’s approved METL. Communication: Utilize both oral and written products to effectively communicate ideas, viewpoints, and military correspondence. Employ active listening and the components of communication to solve problems and achieve results. Exercise and enforce emotional intelligence (self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, and social skill). Operations: Advise commanders and staff; direct the NCO support channel in all operations. Extend the operational reach of the commander in all aspects, from leadership to planning and operations. Analyze operational gaps in both the organization and the plan; leverage the shortfalls by providing the appropriate resources and guidance to achieve positive results. Program Management: Train and educate subordinates on Army and organizational programs. Execute talent management. Assist and enforce the standards associated with unit maintenance programs. Professional Reading: AR 623-3 (Evaluation and Reporting System) AR 600-100 (Army Profession and Leadership Policy) STP 21–24 (Soldier’s Manual of Common Tasks, Warrior Leader Skills Level 2, 3, and 4) AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy) TC 7-21.13 (Soldiers Guide) FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness) TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide) TC 3-09.8 (Fire Support and Field Artillery Certification and Qualification) Individual Critical Task List (ICTL) Skill Level 1, Skill Level 2 ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession) FM 7-0 (Training) ADP 7-0 (Training) ADP 3-19 (Fires) FM 3-0 (Operations) AR 350-1 (Army Training and Leader Development) ADP 6-0 (Mission Command: Command And Control Of Army Forces) Military & Civilian Education: Military Education: Master Leaders Course (MLC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS competencies are vital for preparing yourself and improving your combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: Completing a bachelor’s degree is an excellent way to separate yourself from your peers. For assistance with your educational goals, visit your installation''s Education Center. Competitive Events: Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) Larry L Strickland Educational Leadership Award (SELA) Edmund L. Gruber Award Sergeant Audie Murphy Club (SAMC) Sergeant Morales Club Mentorship & Career Guidance: Establish relationships based on mutual trust and respect. This allows leaders to establish a mentorship role to advise and guide both personal and professional growth. Use ADP 6-22 to refine the mentorship process. To provide strategic career guidance to all subordinate enlisted Soldiers within your formation, reference the information in this pamphlet at each appropriate level. Advise section leadership on talent management, ensuring the right leaders are placed in the right positions to maximize organizational effectiveness. Your role in professional counseling is critical for shaping the future of the NCO Corps.', '07a8c68df95bdb0af21561ba3f212329f771f405287693c6165a2d247d6a5b31', 11, 14, null, 34, null, null, false, null),
  ('163c32f9-68fa-5ab8-a09e-c20cef608394', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13Z', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major / Command Sergeant Major — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Self-Development by Rank', 'The SGM is the key enlisted member of staff elements at battalion and higher levels. The SGM is key in the training and operations planning process, leveraging their experience and vast technical and tactical knowledge to advise the staff and the Operations Officer. The SGM is a subject matter expert in their field, primary advisor on policy development, analytical reviewer of regulatory guidance and often fulfills the duties of the CSM in the incumbent’s absence. The CSM is the senior NCO in the command at battalion and higher levels. The CSM carries out, and enforces, policies and standards on performance, training, appearance and conduct of the organization. The CSM is the principal advisor to the commander, giving advice and making recommendations to the commander and staff on matters pertaining to the organization. The CSM is responsible for enlisted talent management and ensures all aspects of the NCO Common Core Competencies (NCO C3) are present in the operational domain. Core Competencies: Readiness: Determine effectiveness of all policies and practices affecting readiness; manage the process. Manage the personnel and individual training components of the Unit Status Reporting (USR). Manage Human Resources to maintain directed levels of individual readiness. Leadership: Develop agile and adaptive leaders who solve problems using critical and creative thinking to sustain an organizational culture that enables Mission Command. Mentor staff NCOs and officers, continually develop their skills to build a stronger staff and planning cell. Extend influence inside and outside the organization through negotiation and utilizing social intelligence (self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, and social skills). Training Management: Ensure training meets the commander’s intent, mission requirements, and Soldier’s abilities. Foster an organizational learning culture where opportunities for growth and improvement are enduring. Eliminate or minimize training distractions; discipline the training management process. Communication: Utilizes engaging communication techniques and messages to ensure open communication, solicit feedback from the staff, and remains approachable to foster a positive command environment that supports organizational growth through open and candid communication. Engage key stakeholders and foster strong relationships with agencies within the community to build teams and expand the unit’s sphere of influence. Build relationships with peer units and higher headquarters to champion the unit up and out. Operations: Extend the operational reach of the commander in all aspects, from leadership, to planning, and operations. Engage throughout the planning process – Influence planning efforts to reflect reality and true training goals. Ensure leaders are accountable and responsible for the proficiency of their subordinates (Soldiers and Army Civilians) to prepare to conduct its wartime mission. Program Management: Develops, mentors, and manages talent two levels down, based on organizational requirements and assists in subordinates career progression. Shape leaders through talent management efforts that develop NCOs through educational and training opportunities. Enforce effective use of available time to support unit increases in proficiency; actively champion the balance of work/life balance for Soldiers and leaders. Military and Civilian Education: Military Education: Sergeants Major Course (SMC) is your next step in Professional Military Education (PME). Graduating with honors is an excellent way to distinguish yourself from your peers. Additionally, attending courses that enhance your MOS specific individual and collective tasks is vital for preparing yourself and improving your unit''s combat effectiveness. Civilian Education: While not a requirement for promotion, possessing a bachelor’s degree and actively working towards a graduate degree is an excellent way to separate yourself from your peers. For assistance with your educational goals, visit your installation''s Education Center. Army National Guard of the United States (ARNGUS) Army National Guard Field Artillery NCOs are essential to the Total Force and must meet the same high standards of proficiency and readiness as their Active Army counterparts. Your career path and training opportunities are designed to parallel the active component, ensuring seamless integration during military operations. Equivalent Standards & Training: ARNGUS NCOs must possess the same qualifications, capabilities, and training as Active Army Field Artillery NCOs. Career progression and duty assignments are structured to mirror those in the active component, ensuring you are prepared for any mission. Primary Mission & Readiness: Your primary peacetime mission is to sustain individual and collective training to maintain a constant state of readiness for deployment and combat operations. Focus on perfecting your combat skills and developing your subordinates into a lethal and effective Field Artillery unit. Professional Development: The ARNGUS Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development System (NCOPDS) fulfils all professional development and functional area requirements. While your unit assignments may be influenced by geography, your professional growth path aligns with the standards of the entire Field Artillery community. For specific career progression details, reference the associated MOS chapter for your active duty counterparts. Dual Role as a Citizen-Soldier: In addition to your federal mission, you serve your state and community. You may be activated by your state’s governor to provide support during natural or man made disasters or to assist in Homeland Security missions.', 'a5bfca8483d937f2cdf931492b27a6841b6959eb8c2c7d7c1d46fe57231d1a63', 14, 17, null, 35, null, null, false, null),
  ('23e64087-2c50-5215-8b9f-0e6bbed1f9bd', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'The primary development goal for Cannon Crewmember is to become technically and tactically proficient leaders capable of executing Large-Scale Combat Operations (LSCO) in a multi-domain environment. To achieve this, Soldiers must seek and excel in career developmental leadership positions and strive for excellence while attending the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) and functional courses that provide skills critical to the modern battlefield. Following mastery of core competencies, NCOs should pursue broadening assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and skills fundamental to their continued growth and adaptability throughout the career life cycle. To ensure lethality and readiness for high-intensity conflict, Soldiers must make every effort to achieve the highest possible score on the Army Fitness Test (AFT) and weapons qualifications. Furthermore, Soldiers should attend military courses that expand and improve MOS competencies in order to advance their combat effectiveness.', '2ed098a41066858d0add88e23396e36cb97735edf17f10c5ef3eccce1c1630ee', 18, 18, null, 36, null, null, false, null),
  ('eb5312c1-2116-5cda-b06b-54aa753ccddb', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Institutional training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Basic Combat Training (BCT) and Advanced Individual Training (AIT). Graduating BCT/AIT with honors is one of many ways to identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '3e43f028b99b4f49d2216fd655b385214bcda7f1dda379e0ec0087d1f2acaa00', 18, 18, null, 37, null, null, false, null),
  ('b3f33d95-e409-54df-bcbf-a858dca0799b', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Operational Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'The Cannon Crewmember career is centered on four core operational authorizations: Driver, Cannoneer, Assistant Gunner, and Ammunition Specialist. This focus is essential for building a deep, technical foundation in the MOS, mastering assigned equipment, and achieving proficiency in Warrior Tasks and Battle Drills. Technical and tactical expertise is forged and validated during rigorous training events, including Artillery Tables, Combat Training Center (CTC) rotations, and other field exercises. These combined operational and training experiences culminate in the complete mastery of core MOS competencies and all tasks outlined in the Individual Critical Task List (ICTL), which together form the bedrock of unit readiness and lethality.', '3f14606381de9b2dc8e880e6e8e93af4848de2073b0fc2ac3b5543cbf726ca97', 18, 18, null, 38, null, null, false, null),
  ('846ba15d-5a1a-5081-82e9-9913a509ecf7', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Self-development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Cannon Crewmembers who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as Soldier of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Key', '2ed1e8815750223ae044713228c34843773cef3efdfdf2907f43ea2e47eec06e', 18, 18, null, 39, null, null, false, null),
  ('76b63da7-3544-50e8-9885-0c5d9c3b1668', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Training is Ranger School, Field Artillery Weapons Maintenance Course, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Unit Armorer Course, Unit Rail/Air Loading Training Course, Fuel Handlers Course, Ammunition Handler, Field Sanitation Course, Combat Lifesaver Course, Basic Combatives Course (Level 1), and Bus Driver Course. Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', 'b76632326ed7bc75e9aee1f33b488073d918562b28dc4165c23cf3bb447fff6c', 18, 18, null, 40, null, null, false, null),
  ('1d1fbdbf-66b1-5c96-a01c-9d68e80fe65f', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', ': N/A', '2d8ec85f50804016f3c29c88c7101b5c6b37767505a89d921a2bf29a46f9b904', 18, 18, null, 41, null, null, false, null),
  ('ec3c8dd1-b113-5e16-8ecb-5f980dcc415f', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'training', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Basic Leader Course (BLC). Graduating from an NCOPDS course as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandants List will identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '64c67c3edf7e971075aab24d6c38e65d1e32e167e275245eec340930512baf9f', 19, 19, null, 42, null, null, false, null),
  ('0da13ae6-4384-530f-be74-4bf7eaac3545', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Operational Authorization', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'The Gunner and Ammunition Team Chief are the Developmental positions at this grade. The focus at this level is on the operational unit, where sergeants build on their foundational knowledge to become technical and tactical experts while improving their leadership abilities. This dedicated focus is essential for achieving complete mastery of their core competencies.', '805a5c6429f81e1485bd34b98877b106893a3322278f4c0ecdd73c1333c68977', 19, 19, null, 43, null, null, false, null),
  ('1104168c-420a-5cd1-acaf-6644e144497d', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'self_development', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Sergeants who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as NCO of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Key', 'a946340848989ddd2a4ac4056d37dfeeab3cd95d164911b3616610029224a283', 19, 19, null, 44, null, null, false, null),
  ('acfa7edf-31cf-5a1c-8f75-86507238cc4a', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'training', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Training is Ranger School, Field Artillery Weapons Maintenance Course, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Unit Armorer Course, Unit Rail/Air Loading Training Course, CBRN Officer/NCO, Unit Prevention Leader (UPL), TC-AIMS, TC-AIMS Fuel Handlers Course, Ammunition Handler, Field Sanitation Course, License Instructor/License Examiner Course, Combat Lifesaver Course, and Bus Driver Course. Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', '89cb0eed78905b09382f199412f22389c9dbfa5d3582bbfdba3ec2b367d46a20', 19, 19, null, 45, null, null, false, null),
  ('7d51ded2-c630-5594-a5ad-3bab35f2ddeb', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'N/A', 'e2f79e5b60330bba4c289962231b6ba2957d0b14e7deb3110417003c79dea635', 19, 19, null, 46, null, null, false, null),
  ('4377766b-093a-5db3-8e60-05d1d19c11dd', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Advanced Leader Course (ALC). Graduating from an NCOPDS course as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandants List will identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '185cf0cef39bf056027cc04b798293332ef5e07c597deef11cb865f3781cdba7', 19, 19, null, 47, null, null, false, null),
  ('8c4e5739-2696-5462-b1d9-83a17e6f44d7', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'The Howitzer Section Chief is the Career Developmental position at this grade. Gunnery SGT (ABCT) and Ammunition Section Chief are the CLT positions at this grade. These authorizations develop an NCO’s direct leadership, technical, and tactical expertise through practical application.', '837a42eaa9f52d7a85bf542aec044009912cfb2e7364cc226a105598dbd3cfa8', 19, 19, null, 48, null, null, false, null),
  ('2ec22286-2e46-5a2b-9833-9dc537b9d57b', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Staff Sergeants who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as NCO of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Such courses consist of Ranger School, Jumpmaster School, Master Drivers Course, Jungle School, Arctic Soldier Course, Arctic Leaders Course, Air Assault School, Basic Airborne Course, Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator Course, TC-AIMS II Unit Movement I & II, TC-AIMS System Administrator/Database Administrator, Unit Movement Officer, Equal Opportunity Leaders Course (EOLC), Rappel Master, Tactical Combatives Course Level II, ACE/SI. Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', '64cbaee5ec30ba674d449f3e921e2d5ccf67cb590abd2b6d8becc3e27e06d2e6', 19, 19, null, 49, null, null, false, null),
  ('a8827f60-6992-593e-940b-0ff10972e0a4', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Broadening authorizations are crucial to developing leaders with a wide range of experiences and skills who can operate in ever-changing environments. These assignments include Drill Sergeant, Instructor, Small Group Leader (SGL), Training Developer, Recruiter, Soldier Recovery Unit.', '5b1696f9aa4f1127fdd6d4a04b85fcb13fa56340852dab0a833a923cba28ecaf', 20, 20, null, 50, null, null, false, null),
  ('046fd93f-c5fd-52fc-a82d-81b0488f402b', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional training', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Senior Leader Course (SLC). Graduating from an NCOPDS course as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandants List will identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '554afdffc42c68bacaf04179dbb79a2f1b6e30280190cdb4ceadbe9d0282a28f', 20, 20, null, 51, null, null, false, null),
  ('900b0b15-bfc5-5f16-928e-775031591755', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'The Platoon Sergeant is the CD position at this grade. Operations Sergeant/Master Gunner, Gunnery Sergeant (IBCT), Intelligence Sergeant, and Ammunition NCO are the CLT positions at this grade. These authorizations develop an NCO’s direct leadership, technical, and tactical expertise through practical application. The focus at this level is on the operational unit, where SFC build on their foundational knowledge to become technical and tactical experts while improving their leadership abilities.', '7b45754e5e323430088d20c95e30238cab96d872705f8a4cc5e150c11214b426', 20, 20, null, 52, null, null, false, null),
  ('f6289f23-85b6-530e-9f38-3ff7b5cdc1aa', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Sergeant First Class who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as NCO of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Such courses consist of FA Master Gunner Course, Battle Staff NCO Course, Ranger School, Jumpmaster School, Master Drivers Course, Jungle School, Arctic Soldier Course, Arctic Leaders Course, Air Assault School, Basic Airborne Course, Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator Course, Security Managers Course, TC-AIMS II, and Unit Movement Officer II, Rappel Master, Tactical Combatives Course Level II. ACE/SI.', '4e35f2a40e973889c0688e960d32f530f13e1692add260161ae39c2c0c8adf60', 20, 20, null, 53, null, null, false, null),
  ('253783ce-4b5f-562a-acad-334162a2ca07', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-development', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', 'd3355602e81eed6045ecbeab0921e58b8bbd374011e8a6f08f794383adbfdcab', 20, 20, null, 54, null, null, false, null),
  ('17192137-33d1-53ec-b2e2-b5207772061e', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 5. MOS 13B Cannon Crewmember:', 'Broadening Authorizations are crucial in developing leaders with a wide range of experiences and skills who can operate in ever-changing environments. These assignments include Career Management NCO (FAPO), Career Advisor NCO (HRC), Master Gunner Instructor, Military Science Instructor (MSI), OC/T (CTC Locations), Senior Training Developer, TAC NCO, Senior Small Group Leader (SSGL), OC/T (AC/RC), Senior Drill Sergeant, Senior Instructor, Research Develop Test and Evaluation (RDTE), Security Forces Assistance Brigade (SFAB) Master Gunner, Assistant Inspector General, Recruiter, Sexual Assault Response Coordinator (SARC), Equal Opportunity Advisor (EOA).', '9ca972e5fbbd206dedb1d7e82edf2a2c32004b0051a7c097c716d10ca1274d56', 20, 20, null, 55, null, null, false, null),
  ('081466ea-f791-5699-9267-77d3efee949b', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'The primary development goal for Fire Support Specialist is to become technically and tactically proficient leaders capable of executing Large-Scale Combat Operations (LSCO) in a multi-domain environment. To achieve this, Soldiers must seek and excel in career developmental leadership positions and strive for excellence while attending the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) and functional courses that provide skills critical to the modern battlefield. Following mastery of core competencies, NCOs should pursue broadening assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and skills fundamental to their continued growth and adaptability throughout the career life cycle. To ensure lethality and readiness for high-intensity conflict, Soldiers must make every effort to achieve the highest possible score on the Army Fitness Test (AFT) and weapons qualifications. Soldiers should attend military courses that expand and improve MOS competencies in order to advance their combat effectiveness.', 'b1d967e7acb30163dcd4ceec1fc19797300c36ec067aa0fa6de9e8d243e53d64', 21, 21, null, 56, null, null, false, null),
  ('413809c9-430d-506a-9f60-f0e03b6e70f2', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Basic Combat Training (BCT) and Advanced Individual Training (AIT). Graduating BCT/AIT with honors is one of many ways to identify oneself as possessing potential for further promotion.', '70e2569a53e0aa54042ca0d1f22c4630d470db13875e77731ead9d341db4fa78', 21, 21, null, 57, null, null, false, null),
  ('b613aa77-e0f3-56eb-ab10-46d5df1e0c7b', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Operational Authorization', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'The developmental assignment for the Soldier is Fire Support Specialist. The focus of the Soldier’s career should be in the operational unit, building a strong base of technical expertise in equipment, basic MOS skills, and Warrior Tasks and Battle Drills. Technical and tactical expertise is acquired during Artillery Tables, training at a Combat Training Center (CTC), and during tactical exercises. These combined operational and training experiences culminate in the complete mastery of core MOS competencies and all tasks outlined in the Individual Critical Task List (ICTL), which together form the bedrock of unit readiness and lethality.', '6db895e6bd1d2e3785f7661e018ff912431c8f5e484dcd12bfe5424034ff0fb3', 21, 21, null, 58, null, null, false, null),
  ('40387530-3f8a-54ce-9ec9-c891cb364dd7', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Self-Development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Fire Support Specialists who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as Soldier of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to Self-Development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Key Training is Special Operations Terminal Attack Controller (SOTAC) (SOF Only), Joint Fires Observer (JFO) Course, Advanced Field Artillery Tactical Data System (AFATDS) Operators Course, Ranger School, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Combat Life Saver (CLS) Course, Advanced Situational Awareness Training- Basic Course, Drivers Training Course, Basic Combatives Course Level I, Unit Armorer Course, Field Sanitation Course, Fuel Handler Course, Miles Basic Course, and Unit Mail Service Training Course.', '51624d3865035be0e32cd24cbb0344b8afd1d77e98412824cf7f9a021436031f', 21, 21, null, 59, null, null, false, null),
  ('59638d2f-7d4d-5dfe-900b-83f3269a7ce4', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'N/A', 'e2f79e5b60330bba4c289962231b6ba2957d0b14e7deb3110417003c79dea635', 21, 21, null, 60, null, null, false, null),
  ('d7616027-ac8e-5b47-96f7-ecbe8681eaf7', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'training', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Basic Leader Course (BLC). Graduating from an NCOPDS course as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandants List will identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '64c67c3edf7e971075aab24d6c38e65d1e32e167e275245eec340930512baf9f', 22, 22, null, 61, null, null, false, null),
  ('7afcd3c9-490b-5bf9-a84b-39c5239bf0e0', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'The Forward Observer is the developmental assignment at this grade. Fire Support Sergeant is the CLT position at this grade. These authorizations develop an NCO’s direct leadership, technical, and tactical expertise through practical application.', 'a20f45c499319509358f7359c54205ef4d297c9dada2ace3a6e371479a311427', 22, 22, null, 62, null, null, false, null),
  ('38a5f341-2253-5f58-9fb3-341c452ae072', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'self_development', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Sergeants who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as NCO of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Key', 'a946340848989ddd2a4ac4056d37dfeeab3cd95d164911b3616610029224a283', 22, 22, null, 63, null, null, false, null),
  ('c91e2990-3c48-5b61-92e6-721fddc17144', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'training', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Training is Special Operations Terminal Attack Controller (SOTAC) (SOF Only), Joint Fires Observer (JFO) Course, Precision Strike Suite: Collateral Damage Estimation (CDE) Course, Target Mensuration Only (TMO) Course, Weaponeering Course, Mission Command Digital Master Gunner Course, Advanced Field Artillery Tactical Data System (AFATDS) Operators Course, Ranger School, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Rappel Master, FRIES/SPIES Master, Combat Life Saver (CLS) Course, Advanced Situational Awareness Training- Basic Course, Security Manager Course, and Unit Prevention Leader (UPL), Unit Armorer Course, Unit Rail/Air Loading Training Course, CBRN Officer/NCO, TC-AIMS, TCAIMS Fuel Handlers Course, Field Sanitation Course, and License Instructor/License Examiner Course. Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', 'af098ffe41d4bdfef96fdaae1baab2c3e70af5599ee30e73383357aae79ad033', 22, 22, null, 64, null, null, false, null),
  ('cd67d23f-9816-526f-889d-d5573717835a', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-4..E-5', null, 'Corporal/Sergeant — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'N/A', 'e2f79e5b60330bba4c289962231b6ba2957d0b14e7deb3110417003c79dea635', 22, 22, null, 65, null, null, false, null),
  ('e15248af-1524-5941-b3d8-9ed5c2e096b9', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Advanced Leader Course (ALC). Graduating from an NCOPDS course as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandants List will identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '185cf0cef39bf056027cc04b798293332ef5e07c597deef11cb865f3781cdba7', 22, 22, null, 66, null, null, false, null),
  ('79895242-6486-51d7-bcbd-0790c536e0dc', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational Authorizations', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'The Fire Support NCO (CO, TRP) is the career developmental assignment at this grade. Assistant Fire Support NCO and Fire Support Operations NCO at the battalion and higher are the CLT positions at this grade. These authorizations develop an NCO’s direct leadership, technical, and tactical expertise through practical application.', 'c2b3a63a05a0f5f469304ad62b457f2f4e541ff1d2b01eca69b6c7761d522fe8', 22, 22, null, 67, null, null, false, null),
  ('1410852e-520e-5d41-a83e-04d07136f0c0', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Staff Sergeants who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as NCO of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Such courses consist of Special Operations Terminal Attack Controller (SOTAC) (SOF Only), Joint Operational Fires and Effects Course (JOFEC), Joint Firepower Control (JFC) Course, Precision Strike Suite: Collateral Damage Estimation (CDE) Course, Target Mensuration Only (TMO) Course, Weaponeering Course, Fire Support Vehicle and Mission Equipment Package (MEP) Course, Joint Fires Observer (JFO) Course, Mission Command Digital Master Gunner Course, Advanced Field Artillery Tactical Data System (AFATDS) Operators Course, Ranger School, Battle Staff NCO Course, Common Faculty Development - Developer Course (CFD-DC), Common Faculty Development - Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Drill Sergeant School, Recruiter School, Equal Opportunity Leader School, Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator Course, Master Resiliency Trainer (MRT) Course, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Rappel Master, and FRIES/SPIES Master.', '61f4338e72a43edb1a3a9925001c1189528beb25d1aec3eed9236a28a8bde481', 22, 23, null, 68, null, null, false, null),
  ('0bb6a7c5-3e37-51a7-a661-e0bf3a2b913f', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Broadening authorizations are crucial in developing leaders with a wide range of experiences and skills who can operate in ever-changing environments. These assignments include: Special Mission Unit (SMU), AC/RC (Observer/Controller/Trainer), CTC (Observer/Controller),', '86d8d506afd4b479550f23b55286696d3baf6b5c7c44e18c1579305648f9ebbe', 23, 23, null, 69, null, null, false, null),
  ('274b09e2-2429-517c-a6cd-8a1aa3f6a086', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Training Developer, Fire Support NCO (Cyber Warfare Section), Multi Domain Task Force (MDTF), Theater Fires Command (TFC), Training Specialist (FA BOLC), Small Group Leader (SGL), Instructor, Fire Support Sergeant, Research Training Development and Evaluation (RTDE) NCO, Test and Evaluation NCO, Drill Sergeant, and Recruiter. NCOs must successfully complete the required career development assignment before being considered for a broadening assignment. Performance in these positions is a factor when considering promotion potential.', '75e37b0743c291cead32ad477fa3110ed2b963ac488146f516e0d09cd54cc034', 23, 23, null, 70, null, null, false, null),
  ('ddca7f4a-97d4-5140-9f93-5e88b1e326df', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Senior Leader Course (SLC). Graduating from an NCOPDS course as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandants List will identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '554afdffc42c68bacaf04179dbb79a2f1b6e30280190cdb4ceadbe9d0282a28f', 23, 23, null, 71, null, null, false, null),
  ('b5082c37-2eb2-530c-9cac-caae8a95552c', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'The Fire Support NCO (BN, BDE) is the career developmental assignment at this grade. Fire Support Advisor and Fire Support Operations NCO positions at the division and higher are the CLT positions at this grade. These authorizations develop an NCO’s direct leadership, technical, and tactical expertise through practical application. The focus at this level is on the operational unit, where SFCs build on their foundational knowledge to become technical and tactical experts while improving their leadership abilities.', '253c793f3b4e601fd7aa56d0c38a6715dcc899385f735ebb3fd95a80a1ef4372', 23, 23, null, 72, null, null, false, null),
  ('70d0e522-b428-5bfa-a9fa-0b9af9999052', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Sergeant First Class who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivate essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as NCO of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Such courses consist of FA Master Gunner Course, Joint Targeting School, Special Operations Terminal Attack Controller (SOTAC) (SOF Only), Joint Operational Fires and Effects Course JOFEC), Joint Firepower Control Course (JFC), Collateral Damage Estimation (CDE), Target Mensuration Only (TMO), Weaponeering, Fire Support Vehicle and Mission Equipment Package (MEP) Course, Joint Fires Observer (JFO), Ranger School, Battle Staff NCO Course, Common Faculty Development - Developer Course (CFD-DC), Common Faculty Development - Instructor Course (CFD-IC), Drill Sergeant School, Recruiter School, Equal Opportunity Leader Course (EOLC), Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator Course, Master Resiliency Trainer (MRT) Course, Jumpmaster School, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Rappel Master, and FRIES/SPIES Master. Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', '8c86308e46e44cea9469074667c5c09cd72ba25e511e04638f0ec16ade9f95aa', 23, 23, null, 73, null, null, false, null),
  ('934dd1b2-668f-50c0-b7a8-4dcb7de0847e', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13F', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 6. MOS 13F Joint Fire Support Specialist', 'Broadening authorizations are crucial in developing leaders with a wide range of experiences and skills who can operate in ever-changing environments. These assignments include: Special Mission Unit (SMU), Career Management NCO (FAPO), Career Advisor NCO (HRC), Master Gunner Instructor, AC/RC (Observer/Controller/Trainer), CTC (Observer/Controller), Master Gunner, Senior Training Developer, TAC NCO (USMA), Senior Small Group Leader (SSGL), Battlefield Coordination Detachment (BCD), Ground Liaison Officer (GLO), Senior Cyber Observer/Controller NCO, Senior Instructor, Research Training Development and Evaluation (RTDE) NCO, Security Forces Assistance Brigade (SFAB), Multi Domain Task Force (MDTF), Theater Fires Command (TFC), Operations Sergeant/NCO (IO Command), Fire Support NCO, Assistant Inspector General, Sexual Harassment Assault Response Coordinator (SARC), Equal Opportunity Advisor (EOA), Military Science Instructor (MSI), Senior Drill Sergeant, and Senior Recruiter. NCOs must successfully complete the required career development assignment before being considered for a broadening assignment. Performance in these positions is a factor when considering promotion potential. Special Missions Units (SMU): 13F/Z- SSGs, SFCs, MSGs may serve in Special Missions Units (SMU). These assignments are voluntary and require completion of a rigorous, specialized assessment, selection, and training process. SMU assignments are performance-based, and for those NCOs who continue to meet the annual benchmark, reassessment may allow them to remain in these units. Duties of Artillery Soldiers assigned to an SMU are comprised of traditional Infantry and Artillery tactics, techniques, procedures (TTPs), and mission-specific tactics developed for unconventional operations. These duties may include the following: employment of full-spectrum Joint Fire Support at the tactical, operational, and strategic levels; Terminal Attack Control; conduct direct action; integrate compartmented sensitive capabilities and complete missions associated with these capabilities; and coordinate with other services and Government agencies.', 'ef79f69a6b63ad7b7e540602492203d4c9b6af573c8c13049c09bd0eddb6d4f2', 23, 24, null, 74, null, null, false, null),
  ('b9764aa1-cd31-54a0-b8c1-ce70525abbb0', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13J', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 7. MOS 13J Fire Control Specialist:', 'The primary development goal for Fire Control Specialists is to become technically and tactically proficient leaders capable of executing Large-Scale Combat Operations (LSCO) in a multi-domain environment. To achieve this, Soldiers must seek and excel in career developmental leadership positions and strive for excellence while attending the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) and functional courses that provide skills critical to the modern battlefield. Following mastery of core competencies, NCOs should pursue broadening assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and skills fundamental to their continued growth and adaptability throughout the career life cycle. To ensure lethality and readiness for high-intensity conflict, Soldiers must make every effort to achieve the highest possible score on the Army Fitness Test (AFT) and weapons qualifications. Furthermore, Soldiers should attend military courses that expand and improve MOS competencies in order to advance their combat effectiveness.', 'e1f878bce20fef2765284ee7ee6a0746f318c12a3264423bfcfcfa9edcd1a518', 24, 24, null, 75, null, null, false, null),
  ('1b83469c-0933-5f92-9af5-0a7a7151163b', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13J', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Institutional training', 'Chapter 7. MOS 13J Fire Control Specialist:', 'Basic Combat Training (BCT) and Advanced Individual Training (AIT). Graduating BCT/AIT with honors is one of many ways to identify oneself as a top performer with significant potential.', '3e43f028b99b4f49d2216fd655b385214bcda7f1dda379e0ec0087d1f2acaa00', 25, 25, null, 76, null, null, false, null),
  ('d6f3b9a8-3e1b-540a-9f1d-a643b67c0a5a', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13J', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Operational Authorizations', 'Chapter 7. MOS 13J Fire Control Specialist:', 'The Fire Control Specialist career is centered on three core operational authorizations: Tactical Data System Operator, Liaison Specialist and Vehicle Driver. This focus is essential for building a deep, technical foundation in the MOS, mastering assigned equipment, and achieving proficiency in Warrior Tasks and Battle Drills. Technical and tactical expertise is forged and validated during rigorous training events, including Artillery Tables, Combat Training Center (CTC) rotations, and other field exercises. These combined operational and training experiences culminate in the complete mastery of core MOS competencies and all skill level one tasks outlined in the Individual Critical Task List (ICTL), which together form the bedrock of unit readiness and lethality.', 'e35f4872b63a888c3988a4986366b358d81105cf54c49f6184b5b20d08faf6fc', 25, 25, null, 77, null, null, false, null),
  ('8ad89481-a674-510b-8258-b27f169cc331', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13J', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Self-development', 'Chapter 7. MOS 13J Fire Control Specialist:', 'Fire Control Specialist who proactively pursue continuous learning and development cultivates essential leadership skills and practical knowledge. Earning distinctions like the Expert Soldier Badge (ESB) and participation in competitive events such as Soldier of the Quarter/Year demonstrate significant potential and a commitment to self-development. Functional training enhances both individual MOS proficiency and overall unit capabilities. While all Soldiers will not be able to attend all key training courses, they should prioritize courses that align with their unit METL and TOE requirements. Key Training is Ranger School, Basic Airborne Course, Air Assault School, Unit Armorer Course, Unit Rail/Air Loading Training Course, Fuel Handlers Course, Ammunition Handler, Field Sanitation Course, Combat Lifesaver Course, Basic Combatives Course (Level 1), and Bus Driver Course. Self-development opportunities that support individual educational objectives include correspondence courses, professional certifications, and civilian education.', 'f037c59fdc46af00972a05a13e167e17f303581a47ad1eff4eeded2b0715a71c', 25, 25, null, 78, null, null, false, null),
  ('29e4f696-420c-5458-b8cf-4bd697061787', '5a74e8de-3083-5aab-a6d3-b8cb61ab7910', 'Army', 'enlisted', '13J', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private/Specialist — Broadening Authorizations', 'Chapter 7. MOS 13J Fire Control Specialist:', 'N/A.', '190a648f47ed454cf70ff2bd90dc842faae3f1fe19810cedf4bda2fbedb8201e', 25, 25, null, 79, null, null, false, null),
